00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
jason sokol who's asses assistant  professor of history at the university  of new hampshire also author of the book  all eyes are upon us race and politics  from boston to brooklyn the conflicted  soul of the northeast let's talk about  that conflict what what do you talk  about in the book jason um  that that is this conflict when it comes  to race and politics in this part of the  country  well i argue that the north in the  northeast  there have basically been two dueling  traditions of race relations  on the one hand  the cities and states of the north that  i study massachusetts new york and  connecticut  have often leapt ahead of the rest of  the nation in terms of  uh  being able to  forge breakthroughs in interracial  democracy and racial equality that's on  the one hand on the other hand  i show how these cities and states have  been just as deeply committed to  patterns of racial segregation  and  episodes of racism and racial violence  as anywhere else in the country so those  forces have been at war i argue  throughout the north's history and even  up until today i find this so  fascinating because as someone who grew  up in massachusetts and saw a lot of  what you wrote about firsthand and now  living in new york and brooklyn actually  see a very different approach that still  has these kind of two sides to race  relations i was so interested to read  about what you wrote let's start a  little bit with massachusetts because of  course massachusetts has this kind of  reputation as this progressive  stronghold of forward-looking uh on on  race or election race relations lgbt  rights of course but at the same time  boston does have a pretty ugly history  of racism and segregation we see it uh  very much so with sports during the 60s  and 70s  and even if you look at boston a city  that on paper  is relatively diverse when it comes to  racial demographics you don't see that  in so many parts of boston because the  racial diversity is sort of segregated  in particular neighborhoods dorchester  and south boston is it this was very  very present to me and so many people i  talked to have seen the same things  jason that you write about in the book  what other elements of this am i missing  in this kind of superficial analysis of  boston  well  it's true many african-americans  for a long time thought of and still  think of boston  as a racist place as a racist city um  you know bill russell on the one hand  was the first african-american to coach  a basketball team in the nba on the  other hand he called boston a flea  market of racism quote unquote so it it  it didn't have a a good reputation for  african americans  in some ways it still doesn't but if you  look at for instance black politics  massachusetts has been a place where  african americans  have been able to win white votes and to  win major offices i tell the story of ed  brooke who was elected  as the first  african-american to be popularly elected  to the u.s senate ever and that was from  massachusetts in 1966.  um i take the story right up to deval  patrick  elected governor in  massachusetts and then re-elected so i  think  in some moments massachusetts has been  able to make good on the lofty history  and has shown itself  uh willing to  you know elect african americans in a  way you know 1966 ed brooke was elected  in massachusetts that would have been  absurd that any southern state would  have elected an african-american to the  u.s senate so  everything you say is true about the  deep racial segregation in boston it's  also true that boston has been a racist  place exploded in violence over bussing  but we all but we shouldn't forget the  other tradition  uh in which  boston  as a city and massachusetts as a state  has actually been a beacon  at moments small moments but but uh but  i argue important ones that we shouldn't  forget and that we shouldn't  underestimate in a review of your book  the boston globe said all eyes are upon  us is about the sizeable gap between the  north's ideals and its reality do you  think that that's a good way to kind of  contextualize why there may be this  discrepancy that that we see sometimes  in the nor northeast the idea that the  ideals are very strongly very strongly  stated and heard by many but something  makes the reality fall short of those  ideals and if so what do you think is  keeping the reality from falling short  of the ideals  well i think it's true and you know this  is  a lot of what we're talking about here  is white americans and their ability or  their inability to really commit  themselves to what it means to living in  an integrated and a racially equal  nation  so  in terms of  uh the ideals many white americans white  northerners had have always had  a  commitment to racial integration in the  abstract if you ask them  you know are you in favor of racial  equality and racial justice they would  say yes of course of course i am  uh and this is what for instance  massachusetts residents would say in the  1960s but then if you ask them well are  you  committed to sending your  white student to school with african  americans they say well you know it  depends what school  depends exactly how that works out if  you ask them  should they be compelled to sell their  home to an african-american  if that person puts in a bid on their  home they would you know  in the 1960s they would have not  necessarily said yes so there's been a  uh many white  northerners have had a  distinction between what it means to be  for  equality in the abstract and then they  hesitate to take the steps uh  that are really required  to bring that society into being and so  that that's what i think um  is relevant and resonant about this  the notion of the gap between the ideals  and the reality so this i think gets us  pretty smoothly into our our discussion  about new york city and brooklyn and  when i moved to new york city from  massachusetts i was immediately  obviously aware of the fact that as you  walk around in your regular day you see  exponentially more racial diversity  particularly in western massachusetts  where i started my show there is an  almost total lack of racial diversity  you basically just see white people and  initially i thought that that speaks so  well of new york city but then once i  got beyond this kind of superficial  level of i see all sorts of people  you start to realize that as you take a  a subway  from yankee stadium for example through  manhattan and into brooklyn that the the  racial makeup of that train  is initially very diverse and starts to  kind of whiten as you go through  manhattan and then slowly but surely the  the the white population starts to  shrink in relation to who's on the train  as you enter and head into brooklyn into  the out of our outer parts of brooklyn  so give us a sense of of what we really  are seeing in new york where there's  this kind of superficial  uh diversity in integration but we still  have pretty significant uh lines drawn  between neighborhoods don't we  yeah i mean new york has always been  unique  partially because of its size  you know uh  seven eight million people  packed into the city  and  that does allow for a an everyday  interaction between the races that you  don't see in many other places  new york has a reputation from its  founding as a melting pot a multi-ethnic  place ever since the dutch  founded it uh hundreds of years ago  today  you know what you see is also a very  complex reality you're right on the one  hand you have more races in closer  proximity in new york than anywhere else  um even the black community itself is is  complex you have uh caribbean americans  you have african americans originally  from the south you have african  americans  from new york city itself but you also  have entrenched patterns of spatial and  housing segregation as you say and this  uh  this began to take root  during the years of world war ii during  the years of the second great migration  which was when millions of african  americans  came from the south up to northern  cities  they settled in new york city harlem  already had a thriving black community  but for those migrants who didn't end up  in harlem  they were usually corralled into very  specific parts of brooklyn meaning the  neighborhood uh what we know of as  bedford stuyvesant now which is still  the heart of the black community in  brooklyn and as you're on your subway  train this is where you see many of the  african americans um getting on or  getting off right in the bedford  stuyvesant community no question about  it although the amount of gentrification  that's happening in bed stuy is also  incredible in the last five years  right  you know and and and this is an open  question in terms of what's going to  happen to the african-american community  in brooklyn um up against this  gentrification and the  the growing wealth really everywhere in  brooklyn but in those years of the 40s  and 50s  when african-americans came to brooklyn  and settled in bed-stuy  then you had a lot of whites moving out  of central brooklyn and moving to the  outer areas that you're talking about  bay ridge  bensonhurst and those communities became  sort of little white villages  in the middle of the century and then  uh you also had a lot of people starting  to a lot of white people starting to  move to long island and to the suburbs  in these same years um and so my book  does tell the story of how  brooklyn became racially segregated in  this way the irony is that  white people moved to their own  exclusive neighborhoods  in exactly the same years that they  started rooting for jackie robinson so  this is another sort of duality  complexity  that i show in in my book that on the  one hand the white brooklynites can  welcome robinson and proclaim themselves  to be the frontier of interracial  democracy on the one hand  and on the other  they can flee to their own segregated  exclusive neighborhoods where they don't  want to have anything to do with  african-american families  last thing jason in the limited time we  have left if if you had to say what the  the most important or powerful  potential truly unifying force when it  comes to race relations not one that  creates this kind of superficial  openness to racial integration but one  that truly has an impact what would it  be historically speaking  well i think when when communities have  committed themselves  to integrating their neighborhoods and  to integrating their schools  when white people have been willing to  make the quote-unquote sacrifice which  isn't actually any sacrifice  i think that's paid real dividends you  know when communities have committed  themselves to low-income and mixed  housing  and when  school systems have followed through on  radical widespread plans to to make sure  that their schools had actual  uh mixed makeups i i think those  communities have seen  positive returns and they've showed that  that it can be done we aren't we aren't  just fated to a segregated society  let me remind everybody the book is all  eyes are upon us race and politics from  boston to brooklyn we've been speaking  with the book's author professor jason  sokol from university of new hampshire  really great having you on thank you  thanks david

